    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-41181-6",
    "abstract": null,
    "field": "Climate change",
    "problem": "The climate mitigation potential of reducing black carbon (BC) is overestimated by models that use instantaneous radiative forcing, as they fail to account for rapid atmospheric adjustments that largely compensate for BC's warming effect and weaken the actual surface temperature response.",
    "method": "This study uses a climate model to run multiple simulations with perturbed emissions of black carbon and sulphate, separating rapid atmospheric adjustments from slow climate responses to quantify the weak relationship between black carbon's radiative forcing and its effect on surface air temperature.",
    "task": "The task is to investigate the climate mitigation effectiveness of reducing black carbon emissions, using climate simulations to demonstrate why the resulting surface temperature decrease is significantly weaker than would be expected from its strong positive radiative forcing, especially when compared to sulphate aerosols.",
    "model": [
        "MIROC",
        "SPRINTARS",
        "MIROC-SPRINTARS"
    ],
